    <body>
      <Value><![CDATA[## Why Tags Need to Work

When I first added tags to the blog posts, they were just metadata. Stored, indexed, mostly invisible. Then I realized: tags could be a feature, not just a filing system. What if readers could see which topics kept coming back? What if the most-discussed themes literally stood out on the page?

## The Tag Cloud Awakens

That's where the tag cloud comes in. Instead of a boring alphabetical list, I sized each tag by how many times it appeared across the blog. Suddenly, the tags tell a story—they show the landscape of what we've explored together. The biggest tags? The ideas that kept resonating. The smaller ones? Still valuable, but perhaps more niche.

## Discovery Through Frequency

Here's what I learned: readers don't want to hunt through a haystack of tags. They want to see what matters most, what themes kept pulling my attention back. A proportional tag cloud does exactly that. Click a big tag, and you land on a cluster of related posts. Click a smaller one, and you go deeper into the details.

The implementation was simpler than expected. Count the occurrences, calculate a scale, apply font sizes. A few lines of Razor later, and the tags on the blog list page were responsive, clickable, and genuinely useful.

## More Than Decoration

What started as a design flourish became a real discovery tool. The tag cloud isn't there to look pretty—though it does—but its actual job is helping readers navigate the patterns we've built. When something serves both form and function, that's when you know you've got something right.]]></Value>
    </body>
    <excerpt>
      <Value><![CDATA[Transforming blog tags from boring metadata into a visual discovery tool that lets readers follow the threads that matter most.]]></Value>
